<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="PowerPC:BE:32:default:default">
<!--
    32-bit PowerPC (System V ABI) is ILP32: long is 4 bytes and long long is
    8. ppc_32.cspec had no data_organization, so the type factory fell back to
    an 8-byte long (its LP64 default when int is 4). A declared long then took
    a register pair, and an 8-byte value printed as long:
        int8 ln(int8 a) { return CONCAT44((int4)((uint8)a >> 0x20) + 1,(int4)a); }   (bug)
        unsigned long cll(unsigned int a0,unsigned int a1)                           (bug)
          ... (int4)(v1 * 3UL >> 0x20) ...   a 4-byte literal shifted by 32           (bug)
    Stage: P0 knowledge plane (compiler-spec data organization). No option:
    the cspec states the ABI's long_size, a data fix. kuna-ilp32long-mips.xml
    is the same check for MIPS o32 built by gcc.

    Source (clang 14 -target powerpc-linux-gnu -O2 -fno-pic -fno-inline -c;
    .text verbatim below, the three R_PPC_REL24 call sites resolved by hand):
        long ln(long a) { return a + 1; }
        long long ll(long long a) { return a + 1; }
        long pick(long a, long long b, long c) { return a + c + (long)b; }
        long cl(long x) { return ln(x) * 3; }
        long long cll(long long x) { return ll(x) * 3; }
        long cp(long a, long long b) { return pick(a, b, 7) * 2; }
    pick reads a in r3, b in the pair r5:r6 (high word r5) and c in r7.

    Pass 1 (catalog default) decompiles cll before anything is declared, then
    declares ln, ll and pick with C keyword types and decompiles the rest.
    Pass 2 repeats ln, ll and pick with option ctypes on (the CLI rendering).
    Assert #1: cll's recovered 8-byte return spells long long, and its 8-byte
    constant carries the ULL suffix.
    Assert #2: a declared long is 4 bytes; ln adds one to a single register.
    Assert #3: pick takes a 4-byte a and c around the 8-byte b.
    Assert #4: cl and cp pass one register per long.
    Assert #5: with ctypes on the prototypes read int / long long.
    Assert #6: nothing spells an 8-byte integer as unsigned long.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x10000" readonly="true">
